            'authority_name' => 'Dirección General de Migración (DIGEMIG), Bolivia',
            'authority_url' => null,
            'eligibility_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Qualifies</h4><p>A spouse and minor children of a foreign sponsor who holds Bolivian temporary (or permanent) residence are generally described as eligible for a dependent Permanencia Temporal.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Path to Permanent Residence</h4><p>Holders of continuous temporary residence (commonly cited at around 2 years) may become eligible to apply for Permanencia Definitiva at DIGEMIG, which is reported to require an updated police record, proof of income/financial stability, and possibly evidence of integration into Bolivian society.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Refugee Route Is Separate</h4><p>A distinct family-reunification channel exists through CONARE for first-degree relatives of persons already recognised as refugees in Bolivia — this does not apply to the general case of an Indian family member seeking to join a relative who is simply a Bolivian resident.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Relationship Evidence</h4><p>A marriage certificate (for a spouse), legalised and translated into Spanish, is cited by one source as a requirement; birth certificates would presumably be required for children, though this was not independently confirmed.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Unmarried Partners</h4><p>No evidence was found of a route for unmarried partners in Bolivia; cited provisions reference spouses (cónyuges) specifically.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Applying From India</h4><p>Bolivia\'s diplomatic presence in India was not confirmed in search results; confirm the nearest Bolivian consulate handling Indian applications before proceeding.</p>',
