            'government_fee_text' => 'Not independently confirmed in this search',
            'application_centre' => 'Online via the Cancilleria (Ministry of Foreign Affairs) visa portal, or at a Colombian consulate serving India for the principal applicant where relevant',
            'authority_name' => 'Ministerio de Relaciones Exteriores de Colombia (Cancilleria)',
            'authority_url' => 'https://www.cancilleria.gov.co/node/26935',
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Per the Cancilleria\'s official beneficiary-visa page, beneficiaries are defined as: the spouse or permanent partner (compañero/a permanente), and children under 25 who are economically dependent on the principal visa holder. Children over 25 can qualify only with a duly certified disability that prevents financial independence.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Which Principal Visas Can Sponsor a Beneficiary</h4><p>The Cancilleria lists all Visa R (resident) subcategories plus several Visa M (migrant) subcategories, and -- among Visa V (visitor) subcategories -- only: V Estudiante (postgraduate study only), V Religioso, V Voluntario o Cooperante, V Nomada Digital, and V Corresponsal Permanente. An ordinary tourist/visitor Visa V does not appear to qualify as a sponsoring visa for a beneficiary.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Unmarried Partners</h4><p>A permanent partner (compañero/a permanente, i.e. a civil/common-law union) is explicitly included alongside spouses in the official eligibility definition. A secondary legal-firm source adds that the union must be documented/formalised for over one year -- this detail was not independently confirmed against the Cancilleria text itself.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Sponsor (Principal Visa Holder) Requirements</h4><p>Must hold a qualifying principal visa (an R visa, certain M visas, or one of the five specific V subcategories listed above) and must personally submit the beneficiary visa request.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Documents the Sponsor Must Provide</h4><ul><li>Copy of the sponsor\'s own valid visa</li><li>A written declaration accepting economic responsibility for the beneficiary\'s stay, including health coverage</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Documents the Applicant (Beneficiary) Must Provide</h4><ul><li>Civil registry record or marriage certificate proving the relationship or kinship, apostilled/legalised as required for foreign documents</li><li>Valid passport</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What This Means for Indian Families</h4><p>If your relative in Colombia holds only an ordinary tourist/visitor Visa V, they likely cannot sponsor you as a beneficiary -- check which specific visa category they hold first. Our consultants can help confirm whether their visa qualifies and help assemble the apostille/legalisation package for Indian civil documents.</p>',
