            'eligibility_html' => '<p>This is a genuine evidence gap, consistent with earlier-phase findings for this country. No official source was found confirming eligibility criteria for a family-reunion long-term visa, dependent-child age limits, or whether unmarried partners are recognised. One secondary guide mentions that after marrying a Congolese citizen, a foreign national can seek citizenship only after five years of cohabitation -- this concerns citizenship, not a residence/family visa, and should not be conflated with a reunification route.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What Is Reasonably Clear</h4><p>There is no separate "permanent residence" category in the Republic of the Congo -- long-term presence is managed through renewable long-term visas (1/3/5-year options per one secondary source).</p><p>Given the lack of official documentation, we disclose this honestly as a genuine gap rather than guessing at requirements.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Short Visit to Family</h4><p>A Family Visit Visa is referenced for short private stays; one source notes each traveller normally needs their own visa (i.e. no single group/family visa covering multiple relatives). A secondary source lists a EUR 55 fee valid for 15 days for a general visa category, with documents including a passport, photo, invitation letter or accommodation certificate, flight ticket, and proof of funds -- though it is unclear whether this exact fee/validity applies to the family-visit category specifically.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Long-Term Reunification</h4><p>If you want to relocate to join family long-term, the evidence for a dedicated, documented process is essentially absent from available official sources. What is reported is a general long-term visa (1/3/5-year options) that one commercial guide associates with purposes including family reunion, but with no confirmed document list, fee, or eligibility criteria.</p><p>Because this is one of the thinnest-documented countries in this research batch, we strongly recommend direct verification with the Embassy of the Republic of the Congo in New Delhi before proceeding, and our consultants can help initiate that contact.</p>',
