            'biometric_status' => 'unknown',
            'interview_status' => 'unknown',
            'official_visa_name' => 'Resident Spouse Permit / Special Spouse Permit (Cook Islands) -- distinct from New Zealand\'s own immigration system',
            'visa_subclass_code' => null,
            'intro_html' => '<p>The Cook Islands is a self-governing realm of New Zealand but runs its own immigration system through the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Immigration (MFAI), with its own forms and permit categories -- it does not simply mirror mainland New Zealand\'s immigration rules. Officially published MFAI application forms confirm two distinct spouse/partner routes: a Resident Spouse permit (based on a relationship with a Cook Islander, a permanent resident, or a resident investor) and a Special Spouse permit (for a partner of someone on a Government or International Worker visa). A separate short-stay Visitor Permit exists for relatives who simply want to visit, generally issued on arrival to eligible nationalities including, per secondary sources, Indian passport holders.</p>',
            'typical_stay' => 'Not independently confirmed for the Resident/Special Spouse permits. The Visitor Permit is reported by secondary sources as issued on arrival, subject to meeting entry conditions, with extensions handled via a separate visitor-permit extension form.',
            'entry_type' => 'Resident Spouse / Special Spouse permit (residence-linked, tied to the partner\'s own status) versus a short-stay Visitor Permit (for family visits)',
            'processing_time_text' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'validity_text' => 'Not independently confirmed -- one secondary aggregator (Visa Atlas) states the Resident Spouse and Special Spouse permits require an approved sponsor and do not lead to permanent residence, but this was not independently verified against the official MFAI forms',
            'application_method' => 'Official MFAI application forms (Resident Spouse / Special Spouse Visa and Permit application, and a separate Sponsorship Application form) filed with the Cook Islands Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Immigration',
            'interview_required' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'biometric_required' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'government_fee_text' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'application_centre' => 'Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Immigration (MFAI), Cook Islands (mfai.gov.ck)',
            'authority_name' => 'Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Immigration (MFAI), Cook Islands',
            'authority_url' => 'https://mfai.gov.ck',
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Per the official MFAI Spouse Visa and Permit application form, there are two distinct partner routes: (1) Resident Spouse permit, based on a relationship with a Cook Islander, a permanent resident, or a resident investor; and (2) Special Spouse permit, based on being the partner of someone applying for or holding a Government or International Worker visa. The form specifically asks whether dependents are also applying alongside the partner.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Sponsor Requirements</h4><p>Per MFAI\'s Sponsorship Application form and the Immigration Regulations 2023 (regulation 45), a sponsor can be a person with continuing right of residence, or a person who holds a resident, work, or investor permit and currently resides in the Cook Islands. Sponsors have obligations covering accommodation standards and financial support, per the Immigration Regulations 2023.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Dependent Children</h4><p>A visitor-permit extension form notes that "family" in that context may include a principal applicant, partner, and dependent children under 18 -- this age threshold is specific to the visitor-permit context and was not confirmed as applying identically to the Resident/Special Spouse permits.</p><p>This route-specific detail was drawn from official MFAI forms referenced in search snippets; the full regulatory text (Immigration Regulations 2023) should be checked directly for complete current requirements.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Visiting Family (Short Stay)</h4><p>Indian passport holders are reported by secondary sources as not needing a visa in advance for a straightforward family visit -- a Visitor Permit is typically issued on arrival, subject to entry conditions (passport valid 6+ months beyond stay, a return/outbound ticket, and proof of accommodation).</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Joining a Spouse/Partner Long-Term</h4><p>You would use the Resident Spouse permit (if your partner is a Cook Islander, permanent resident, or resident investor) or the Special Spouse permit (if your partner holds a Government or International Worker visa) -- these are the official MFAI forms, and the correct one depends entirely on your partner\'s own status.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Sponsor-Side Requirements (Kept Separate From Your Documents)</h4><p>Your partner/sponsor must already hold continuing residence rights or a resident/work/investor permit, and meet MFAI\'s accommodation and financial obligations as sponsor.</p><p>Because the Cook Islands runs distinct immigration rules from mainland New Zealand, do not assume NZ visa-waiver or residence rules apply here -- our consultants can help identify which MFAI permit form fits your specific relationship and help you liaise with MFAI directly.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Cook Islands Spouse & Family Permit Guide for Indians',
            'meta_description' => 'Joining a partner or visiting family in the Cook Islands from India? Learn the Resident/Special Spouse permit routes under MFAI, via VisaAgency.in.',
