            'intro_html' => '<p>Costa Rica\'s Direccion General de Migracion y Extranjeria (DGME) does recognise family-tie-based residency, but the evidence found is strongest for relatives of a Costa Rican citizen (vinculo), not clearly for relatives of a foreign resident. First-degree relatives of a Costa Rican citizen -- reported by secondary sources to include spouses, parents, children (minors or disabled adults), and minor or disabled siblings -- can seek permanent residency without the usual 3-year wait otherwise required. Separately, dependent-employee temporary residence categories (for specialised/dependent workers) carry their own spouse and child provisions, requiring legalised/apostilled marriage certificates. Whether a spouse of a foreign (non-Costa Rican) resident has an equivalent dedicated "vinculo" route was not confirmed in this search -- this is a specific, named gap.</p>',
