            'application_centre' => 'Direccion General de Migracion y Extranjeria (DGME), Costa Rica, in-country; for the short-stay family-visit route, a Costa Rican embassy/consulate serving India',
            'authority_name' => 'Direccion General de Migracion y Extranjeria (DGME), Costa Rica',
            'authority_url' => null,
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Secondary sources describe permanent residency by vinculo (family tie) with a Costa Rican citizen as open to parents, children (minors or adults with a qualifying disability), and minor or disabled siblings -- with one secondary (non-official) source adding spouses of citizens to this same fast-track, bypassing the usual 3-year wait. DGME is reported to cross-check the relationship against Supreme Electoral Tribunal civil-registry records, and name-spelling mismatches between documents are flagged as a common cause of rejection.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Gap: Spouse of a Foreign (Non-Costa Rican) Resident</h4><p>We did not find confirmed DGME requirements for a spouse joining a foreign resident (as opposed to a Costa Rican citizen) under a dedicated family-tie category -- the clearest spouse/child provisions found belong instead to the dependent-employee temporary-residence categories (for specialised technicians, executives, managers, etc.), which require a certified, legalised or apostilled marriage certificate for the spouse, with minors\' applications filed by their parents and adult disabled children requiring a medical opinion.</p><p>Given this specific gap, confirm directly with DGME or a Costa Rican immigration attorney whether an equivalent vinculo-style route exists for spouses of foreign residents.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>If Your Relative Is a Costa Rican Citizen</h4><p>You may be able to use the vinculo route for permanent residency (bypassing the standard 3-year wait), per secondary sources, if you are a parent, child, or (per one non-official source) spouse of that citizen. The core evidence is the civil registry record linking you, checked against Supreme Electoral Tribunal records -- keep your name spelling exactly consistent across all documents.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>If Your Relative Is a Foreign Resident (Not a Costa Rican Citizen)</h4><p>If you are the spouse of a foreign national who holds Costa Rican temporary residence as a dependent employee (specialised technician, executive, etc.), you would likely fall under that category\'s own spouse/child provisions, requiring a certified, legalised/apostilled marriage certificate. A dedicated "vinculo"-style route specifically for spouses of foreign (non-citizen) residents was not confirmed -- check this gap directly with DGME.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>If Only Visiting Family</h4><p>Most sources describe Indian citizens as needing a standard visa to enter Costa Rica (via a Costa Rican embassy/consulate), with a legalised invitation letter from your host useful if staying with family -- though one source claims visa-free entry for up to 90 days, conflicting with the majority view. Confirm current requirements before booking travel.</p><p>Our consultants can help clarify which category -- vinculo, dependent-employee family provision, or short-stay visit -- actually fits your situation, and can help with the DGME consular-registration step introduced from 1 July 2025.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Costa Rica Family-Tie Residency Guide for Indians',
            'meta_description' => 'Joining or visiting family in Costa Rica from India? Understand the vinculo route, dependent-employee family provisions, and a key evidence gap, via VisaAgency.in.',
