            'government_fee_text' => 'Not independently confirmed for the family-reunification residence route; the Schengen short-stay visa fee follows the standard EU Schengen visa fee schedule',
            'application_centre' => 'Schengen visit visa: Croatian consulate or outsourced visa application centre in India. Family reunification residence permit: competent police administration (policijska uprava) in Croatia, after an application is lodged there or via a Croatian diplomatic mission.',
            'authority_name' => 'Ministry of the Interior (MUP), Republic of Croatia',
            'authority_url' => 'https://mup.gov.hr/aliens-281621/temporary-stay-281662/281662',
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Family reunification in Croatia is available to third-country national family members of: (a) a Croatian citizen, (b) a third-country national who holds long-term residence or international protection in Croatia, or (c) a third-country national on a one-year stay-and-work permit, provided the family member already has at least 1 year of temporary stay. Eligible family includes spouses and, per the official guidance, non-marital (unmarried) partners — though reunification is refused if the sponsor or applicant is already married to or in a long-term relationship with someone else, or if the marriage is found to be one of convenience.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Sponsor Requirements</h4><ul><li>The sponsor must already hold the qualifying Croatian residence status described above.</li><li>Sufficient means of subsistence and suitable accommodation for the family are required; a precise current income figure was not independently confirmed in this research (an older, pre-euro 2016 EMN figure exists but is outdated and not reliable for current use).</li></ul><p>This is general guidance only — every application is individually assessed by Croatian immigration authorities.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Two Different Routes</h4><p>Do not assume a tourist/Schengen visit visa lets you move in with family long-term in Croatia — a short family visit and family-reunification residence are handled under different rules. If you only want to visit, you generally apply for a Schengen Type C visa through a Croatian consulate/visa centre in India, supported by an invitation letter and proof of the relationship. If you want to live in Croatia with a resident or citizen family member, you (or your sponsor) must apply for temporary stay for family reunification.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Documents Likely Needed</h4><p>Valid passport (6+ months beyond the stay), relationship evidence (marriage/birth certificate, likely translated and apostilled/legalised, though this was not independently confirmed), proof of sponsor\'s residence status, and proof of accommodation and means of subsistence.</p><p>Because Croatia\'s current income thresholds and document checklist were not independently confirmable via search, our consultants can help you verify the latest requirements directly with the Croatian authorities before you apply.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Croatia Family Visa for Indians: Visit vs Reunification',
            'meta_description' => 'Joining family in Croatia from India? Compare the Schengen family-visit visa with Croatia\'s national family-reunification residence permit, via VisaAgency.in.',
