            'validity_text' => 'Short-stay visa: single trip, within the stay limit noted above. Rib\'e Lugá permit: now capped so that a dependent\'s permit cannot run longer than the main applicant/sponsor\'s own permit (rule effective 1 April 2026); it is described as not leading to permanent residence.',
            'application_method' => 'Short-stay visa: applied for at a Netherlands Embassy/Consulate (e.g. in India), reportedly at least one month before travel. Rib\'e Lugá: applied for in Curacao with an approved sponsor, through the Admission Office (Toelatingsorganisatie)/immigrationcur.org channel.',
            'interview_required' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'biometric_required' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'government_fee_text' => 'Short-stay visa: sources conflict, citing both approximately EUR 80 and an older EUR 35 figure — confirm the current fee on netherlandsworldwide.nl. Rib\'e Lugá: fees must be paid in advance for each year of validity requested; exact amount not independently confirmed.',
            'application_centre' => 'Short-stay visa: Netherlands Embassy/Consulate in India (or nearest Dutch representation). Rib\'e Lugá: Toelatingsorganisatie (Admission Office), Curacao, applied for in-country by/with the sponsor.',
            'authority_name' => 'Toelatingsorganisatie Curacao (Admission Office) / Netherlands Worldwide (netherlandsworldwide.nl)',
            'authority_url' => 'https://www.immigrationcur.org/en/ribe-luga/purpose-family-reunification-family-formation/',
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>For a short family VISIT, an Indian national generally needs the Dutch Caribbean short-stay visa; a letter of invitation from the Curacao-based family member is typically requested as part of the entry requirements, alongside proof of accommodation, return/onward travel, and sufficient funds. This visa does not permit paid work and is not valid for the European Netherlands or other Schengen countries.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Family Reunification (Rib\'e Lugá)</h4><p>This route is a temporary residence permit for dependent family members, for the purpose of family reunification or family formation. It requires an approved sponsor already resident in Curacao before the dependent applies, and it is described as not leading directly to permanent residence. A related but separate route (Declaration Van Rechtswege, for family reunification with a Dutch citizen specifically) cites a minimum sponsor income of around XCG 1,900 gross per month for a spouse as main applicant, with additional amounts for children by age — this figure was found for that related route, not confirmed as the Rib\'e Lugá figure itself, so treat it as indicative only.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Recent Rule Change (1 April 2026)</h4><p>Dependents\' permits can no longer run longer than the sponsor\'s own permit, and incomplete applications no longer get a grace period to add missing documents — all required documents must be submitted at filing.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Visiting vs Living With Family</h4><p>Do not assume the short-stay visit visa lets you live with family in Curacao long-term — it is a separate, more limited document. If you want to live with a Curacao-resident family member, you and your sponsor need to apply under Rib\'e Lugá, and your sponsor must already hold an approved immigration status in Curacao.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Short Visit Documents</h4><p>Passport, invitation letter from your family member, proof of accommodation, return/onward ticket, proof of funds, and the Digital Immigration Card completed online before departure.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Family Reunification Documents</h4><p>Not fully confirmed in this research pass — the official checklist sits on a separate "List of Requirements" tab on immigrationcur.org that we could not open directly. Expect relationship evidence and proof of your sponsor\'s status and resources at minimum.</p><p>Our consultants can help confirm the current Rib\'e Lugá document checklist and sponsor income requirement with the Admission Office before you apply.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Curacao Family Visa for Indians: Visit vs Reunification',
