            'eligibility_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Route 1: Family Reunification</h4><p>For the spouse and minor (under-18, unmarried) children of a third-country national sponsor. Sources indicate the sponsor typically needs about 2 years of lawful residence in Cyprus and a residence permit valid for at least 1 year. A secondary (non-official) source cites a sponsor income figure of around €2,500 gross per month, rising per dependent, plus stable housing and health insurance — this figure is not independently confirmed as the current official threshold. If the spouse does not yet meet full conditions (e.g. passport validity or marriage duration issues), a temporary residence permit for "Dependent Visitors" may be issued instead, per the Migration Department.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Route 2: Pink Slip Dependents</h4><p>A non-working, financially self-sufficient resident (holding the "Pink Slip" temporary residence permit) can bring a spouse and children under 18 as dependents, each submitting a separate application and receiving their own card. The main applicant must show sufficient annual foreign income — commonly cited by law firms as at least €24,000 — to cover the family\'s living costs in Cyprus; this visitor-type permit does not allow employment.</p><p>No source in this pass confirmed recognition of unmarried/de-facto partners under either route — both describe only spouses and minor children as eligible dependants.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Which Route Applies to You</h4><p>If your Cyprus-based family member is a working third-country national resident, you would fall under Family Reunification; if they are a financially self-sufficient, non-working resident on a Pink Slip, you would apply as their dependent under that permit instead. These have different sponsor-income expectations and should not be conflated.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Documents Likely Needed</h4><p>Passport, marriage/birth certificate (translated/legalised — exact legalisation requirements not independently confirmed), proof of the sponsor\'s residence status and income, and proof of accommodation and health insurance.</p><p>Because Cyprus\'s exact current income thresholds and permit durations conflict across sources, our consultants can help confirm the figures that apply to your specific sponsor category with the Civil Registry and Migration Department before you apply.</p>',
