            'authority_url' => 'https://gis.gov.gh/',
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Eligibility is limited to a legally married spouse and minor children; per one commercial guide reproducing GIS\'s own dependant-permit page, Ghana does not recognise life partnerships or same-sex relationships for immigration purposes.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Single-Source, Unverified Marriage-Duration Rule</h4><p>One source states that spouses are eligible for this permit only when married for more than 4 years. No other source found in this research repeats this condition, so it should be treated as requiring direct verification with GIS rather than relied on outright.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Sponsor Requirement</h4><p>The sponsor (principal applicant) must already possess a valid Residence Permit, or at minimum a Residence Permit receipt issued by GIS, before a dependant application can be made.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Document Legalisation</h4><p>All documents issued outside Ghana (such as an Indian marriage certificate) must be authenticated and, where necessary, translated into English.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies</h4><p>The Dependant Residence Permit is filed in-country with GIS, generally once your Ghana-based sponsor already holds a valid Residence Permit; it does not authorise you to work in Ghana.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Documents You Will Likely Need</h4><ul><li>Valid passport</li><li>Marriage certificate (authenticated/apostilled, and translated into English if not already in English)</li><li>Proof that your sponsor can support you financially</li><li>Non-Citizen ID Card registration if staying beyond 90 days</li></ul><p>Our consultants can help confirm current authentication/apostille requirements for Indian documents and the single-source 4-year marriage-duration claim directly with GIS before you apply.</p>',
