            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Per the Danish Immigration Service\'s own guidance, family reunification in Greenland can be granted where the applicant is a spouse, registered partner, or cohabiting partner (among other categories) of someone living in Greenland — notably, Greenland\'s official route explicitly includes unmarried cohabiting partners alongside spouses and registered partners, rather than restricting the route to marriage only.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Sponsor Requirement</h4><p>The sponsoring spouse/partner must already be living in Greenland. The official guidance states there are further specific requirements relating to the sponsor living in Greenland, but the detailed income/housing criteria were not available in the search results reviewed for this research.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Visit vs Residence</h4><p>Holding a Danish (mainland) residence permit does not, by itself, allow entry to Greenland — a separate Greenlandic entry permit is required even for a short visit, holiday, or family visit. This confirms that the short-stay entry-permit process (confirmed in a prior phase of this project) and the Family Reunification Residence Permit described here are two distinct routes within the same overall Danish/Greenlandic system.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Visit vs Reunification — Do Not Conflate Them</h4><p>If you simply want to visit a family member living in Greenland for a short stay, that is the separate Greenlandic entry-permit/ApplyVisa process already confirmed in an earlier phase of this project. If you want to join a spouse, registered partner, or cohabiting partner living in Greenland on a long-term residence basis, that is this distinct Family Reunification Residence Permit route.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Where You Apply</h4><p>You and your partner each complete a GL/FA1a form as part of the GL/FA1 packet, and you submit it with your documents to the Danish embassy/consulate in India.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What Your Sponsor Needs</h4><p>Your sponsor must already be living in Greenland and meet further requirements that were not fully detailed in the sources reviewed — our consultants can help you confirm the current income/housing conditions with the Danish Immigration Service before applying.</p>',
